      "canonicalConceptId": "concept:8ef921651c583d482b236c5b5df6097d660e5ad21be70a84f29196ec6520d6df",
      "text": "Cell replacement for Parkinson's disease transplants dopaminergic progenitor cells derived from embryonic or induced pluripotent stem cells. Recent early-phase, open-label trials of an embryonic stem cell product and an induced pluripotent stem cell product report safety findings plus exploratory motor and imaging changes, and their authors caution about small cohorts, the absence of a control group and placebo effects. In one trial the authors state that risks were primarily associated with the immunosuppression regimen. A randomized, sham surgery-controlled phase three trial of the embryonic stem cell product is registered as recruiting, and a sponsor announced a conditional, time-limited approval in Japan of an induced pluripotent stem cell product.",
